.TH FLFORMAT 8 "November 25, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
flformat \- format diskettes (2.11BSD)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flformat
[
.B \-f
.I size
]
special
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I flformat
program formats a diskette in the specified drive
associated with the special device
.IR special .
.I Special
is normally /dev/rfl0, for drive 0, or /dev/rfl1,
for drive 1.  The ``raw'' device must be used.
.PP
The diskette is formatted as the largest the drive takes:
1.44 megabytes (18 sectors on a track) in a 3.5-inch drive and
1.2 megabytes (15) in a 5.25-inch one.
With
.B \-f
it is formatted as the
.I size
given in kilobytes: 720, a double density diskette in a 3.5-inch
drive (9 sectors on a track), 1440 or 1200.
.PP
Before formatting a diskette
.I flformat
prompts for verification if standard input is a tty
(this allows a user to cleanly
abort the operation; note that
formatting a diskette will destroy any existing data).
Formatting is done by the driver, a track at a time.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`No such device' means that the drive is not ready,
usually because no diskette is in the drive.
`Invalid argument' means that the drive does not take
diskettes of the size asked for.
`Read-only file system' means that the diskette is protected.
Other error messages are selfexplanatory.
.SH FILES
/dev/rfl?
.SH SEE ALSO
fl(4), rxformat(8)
.SH BUGS
What was written is not read back.
